WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.819
[MUSIC]

00:00:07.819 --> 00:00:12.715
In recent times, there have been massive
investments by companies and

00:00:12.715 --> 00:00:17.122
firms in internal databases and this has
provided us the means to

00:00:17.122 --> 00:00:22.037
provide an unprecedented look at the
behavior of employees.

00:00:22.037 --> 00:00:26.950
The interaction of those employees with
the firm's customers and

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:30.957
a new way to think about and quantify
employee productivity.

00:00:30.957 --> 00:00:35.073
So my research is about taking these very
large,

00:00:35.073 --> 00:00:39.199
big data sets on the internal organization
of firms.

00:00:39.199 --> 00:00:42.113
To better understand employee
productivity,

00:00:42.113 --> 00:00:45.608
to better understand the design of
intensive systems and

00:00:45.608 --> 00:00:49.830
to better understand how these two aspects
can be brought together to

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:53.418
improve the interaction of a company with
its customers.

00:00:53.418 --> 00:00:56.538
[MUSIC]

00:00:56.538 --> 00:00:59.339
There are two kinds of data that we're
talking about here.

00:00:59.339 --> 00:01:02.219
One is on the internal behavior of firms.

00:01:02.219 --> 00:01:07.322
Off the type routinely collected by Human
Resources Management Systems or

00:01:07.322 --> 00:01:10.520
off the type collected routinely by ERP
Systems or

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:13.264
Enterprise Resource Planning Software,

00:01:13.264 --> 00:01:16.788
which collects data on what your employees
are doing.

00:01:16.788 --> 00:01:19.068
How they're spending time on their
activities?

00:01:19.068 --> 00:01:22.477
And combining this with compensation data.

00:01:22.477 --> 00:01:25.977
For example, the salaries, quotas,
commissions.

00:01:25.977 --> 00:01:30.619
Putting these two aspects together, we can
ask, which salaries work?

00:01:30.619 --> 00:01:32.499
Which quotas work?

00:01:32.499 --> 00:01:35.699
Which bonuses work for which sales agent?

00:01:35.699 --> 00:01:39.379
And asked the more broader question,
should we have bonuses at all?

00:01:39.379 --> 00:01:40.559
Do quotas work well?

00:01:40.559 --> 00:01:43.478
[MUSIC]

00:01:43.478 --> 00:01:47.859
Quotas and bonuses and commissions have
very good incentive properties,

00:01:47.859 --> 00:01:51.697
because they give you a lot of incentives
to put in a lot of effort.

00:01:51.697 --> 00:01:56.617
Because they form a goal that you have to
work towards in order to earn commissions.

00:01:56.617 --> 00:02:03.260
While that is true, the flip side is many
people understand that quotas and things

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:09.179
like these also have some bad properties
related to gaming or timing issues.

00:02:09.179 --> 00:02:12.746
If sales people are gaming the quota in
this manner,

00:02:12.746 --> 00:02:17.297
it can actually create some really bad
behavior and distortions.

00:02:17.297 --> 00:02:22.731
In the past in theory, it was thought that
these distortions are interesting,

00:02:22.731 --> 00:02:25.337
but intellectually a curiosum.

00:02:25.337 --> 00:02:28.177
Maybe not that important in the real
world.

00:02:28.177 --> 00:02:32.534
Some of the insights that we have been
able to generate using real data from

00:02:32.534 --> 00:02:36.979
real companies is that the magnitudes of
these distortions can be so huge.

00:02:36.979 --> 00:02:42.355
That the positive effects of quotas and
bonuses may well be overwhelmed if they're

00:02:42.355 --> 00:02:47.679
not designed correctly, because these
distortions just have a huge magnitude.

00:02:47.679 --> 00:02:50.618
[MUSIC]

00:02:50.618 --> 00:02:52.198
Different people are different.

00:02:52.198 --> 00:02:57.018
There is lot of heterogeneity among
salespeople, amongst your employees.

00:02:57.018 --> 00:03:01.608
And it's not likely to be the case that
there is a one size fit,

00:03:01.608 --> 00:03:03.119
fits all approach.

00:03:03.119 --> 00:03:07.595
The value of the data is that we can
actually measure heterogeneity in

00:03:07.595 --> 00:03:09.999
employees employee's skill.

00:03:09.999 --> 00:03:11.969
And we can look at the data and

00:03:11.969 --> 00:03:15.758
say that this employee in this particular
territory.

00:03:15.758 --> 00:03:18.697
For every time he called on a particular
doctor,

00:03:18.697 --> 00:03:22.078
his conversion of a call into sales volume
is very high.

00:03:22.078 --> 00:03:26.303
While some other employee when he called
on a doctor for

00:03:26.303 --> 00:03:31.639
a same number of calls, his conversion or
production of sales is very low.

00:03:31.639 --> 00:03:36.351
So we have the ability to use the data to
fine tune contracts to

00:03:36.351 --> 00:03:38.759
the individual employee level.

00:03:38.759 --> 00:03:42.577
For example, certain kinds of employees
who are quote unquote,

00:03:42.577 --> 00:03:46.398
high powered might prefer that most of
their compensation is

00:03:46.398 --> 00:03:49.748
coming from commissions as opposed to
straight salary.

00:03:49.748 --> 00:03:50.541
So if you hi,

00:03:50.541 --> 00:03:55.527
hire the right kind of employees, you
could make a commission-based system work

00:03:55.527 --> 00:03:59.659
much better than if you have employees who
prefer straight salary.

00:03:59.659 --> 00:04:04.212
On the other hand, if you are forced
because of institutional or

00:04:04.212 --> 00:04:09.080
within company considerations that you can
have only a particular kind of

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:14.026
incentive scheme, you might think, well, I
need to have the right kind of

00:04:14.026 --> 00:04:17.738
employees in order to make this incentive
scheme work.

00:04:17.738 --> 00:04:22.058
What I'm trying to say here is that the
hiring and firing of employees,

00:04:22.058 --> 00:04:26.739
as well as the design of the incentives to
incent them have to go hand and hand.

00:04:26.739 --> 00:04:36.739
[MUSIC]

